{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ada7215e9cdbec94d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TfYwPSgaq8ZH6nJTFDz7zX","status":"completed","output_file":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-filipepinto-Projects-curriculum-builder--plan26-run-plan26-attempts-N50-ADVERSARIAL-REGRESSION-attempt-20260812T150242-611535Z-191331b827-repo/913d8145-a69b-41ae-866d-8d539cb6c5e2/tasks/ada7215e9cdbec94d.output","summary":"# Research Report: tests/runtime/test_plan26_adversarial.py (24 rows, spec 17.2)\n\n## 0. Structural findings (apply to all 24 rows)\n\n**No `conftest.py` anywhere in `tests/` or `tests/runtime/`** (confirmed via `find`). Every `test_plan26_*.py` file is self-contained: each defines its own private (`_`-prefixed) fixtures, fake-transport class, `_Context`/`_HarnessContext` double, and manifest/state builders — duplicated per file rather than shared. `tests/runtime/test_plan26_workbook.py`'s docstring explicitly says it follows \"the convention `test_plan26_unit_graph.py` already established\" for a `test_blocked_*` gap pattern — meaning: copy the *pattern*, don't import the *module*. No test file currently imports another `test_plan26_*` module. `tests/runtime/__init__.py` exists (so cross-file import is technically possible, e.g. `from tests.runtime import test_plan26_unit_graph as UG`), but it would break the established convention — flag this as a decision point rather than assuming it's safe.\n\nAll hash-locked-environment test files open with the same skip guard:\n```python\ntry:\n    import langgraph\n    import langgraph.checkpoint.sqlite\nexcept ModuleNotFoundError as exc:\n    raise unittest.SkipTest(\"plan26 hash-locked environment not installed ...\") from exc\n```\n`test_plan26_adversarial.py` must include this same guard if it touches `langgraph`/`persistence`/`unit_graph`/`graph`.\n\n**The one full end-to-end harness** (real compiled graph, real `StateGraph`, scripted-but-schema-valid model transport) lives in `tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py`, all private:\n- `_build_episode_fixture(tmp_path, units=1) -> dict` — builds engine_root/curriculum/manifest/output_root/sandbox on disk, copies real engine contracts (`ENGINE_CONTRACTS`).\n- `_prepare_episode(fixture, *, mode=\"one\", requested=\"U001\") -> (lock, invocation, envelope)` — real `P.ExecutionLock`, real `P.prepare_episode_invocation`, computes `frozen_digest` via `inputs.D01_VALIDATE_AND_FREEZE_INPUTS`.\n- `_run_episode(monkeypatch, fixture, *, interrupt_after=None) -> dict` with keys `trace`, `updates`, `deferred_frontier`, `state`, `context`, `invocation`, `compiled` — monkeypatches `G.build_model_node_context` to return `_scripted_model_context(sandbox)`, then really calls `G.build_curriculum_factory_graph(...)`, and streams it (`compiled.stream(..., stream_mode=\"updates\", context=context)`), tripping `context.signal_token.trip()` after a named node if `interrupt_after` is given.\n- `_HarnessContext(engine_root, output_root, sandbox)` — `RuntimeContext`-shaped double with real `ArtifactStore`/`EvidenceStore`, `_StubRegistry` (renderer/rasterizer/capability double), `_StubRetriever`, `_SwitchableToken` (`.trip()`/`.is_set()`).\n- `_ScriptedFakeTransport(mn.tp.FakeCliTransport)` + `_scripted_candidate(job_id, projection)` — produces schema-valid canned candidates per job id, still routed through real schema validation (it *is* a `FakeCliTransport`).\n- `REACHABLE_BOUNDARIES`, `UNOBSERVABLE_BOUNDARY`, `MODEL_NODE_IDS = frozenset(mn.MODEL_NODE_ADAPTERS)`.\n\nA **second, sanctioned (non-private) fake-context builder** exists directly in production code: `mn.build_test_model_node_context(sandbox_root=..., responses={...})` in `runtime/langgraph_factory/model_nodes.py`, used e.g. at `tests/runtime/test_plan26_model_nodes.py:1113`. It's simpler (one canned response per job) and is guarded by `_assert_production_transport` so it can never leak into `build_model_node_context` (proven by `test_the_production_context_refuses_a_fake_transport`, `test_only_the_named_test_builder_and_its_guard_mention_the_fake_transport`, `model_nodes.py:1085`).\n\n**SIGINT/crash injection** happens in two different, non-overlapping ways:\n1. In-process, cooperative: `_SwitchableToken`/`P.InterruptToken` — `context.signal_token.trip()` after a node returns; the *next* node boundary observes it via `routing.interrupt_requested`. Used throughout `test_plan26_unit_graph.py` (`_run_episode(..., interrupt_after=...)`) and `test_plan26_persistence.py::TestGracefulInterrupt` (`P.InterruptToken(...).install()`, then literally `signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT)` / `SIGTERM`).\n2. Real OS-level SIGKILL via a **child subprocess**: `tests/runtime/test_plan26_persistence.py` has `_run_child(script, *args)` which writes `script` (a `textwrap.dedent`-ed Python source, e.g. `CRASH_CHILD`, `LOCK_CHILD`, `ORPHAN_CHILD`) to a temp file and runs it with `subprocess.run([sys.executable, child_path, str(REPO_ROOT), *args], ...)`; the child kills itself with `os.kill(os.getpid(), signal.SIGKILL)` at a chosen boundary string (`\"before_admit\"`/`\"after_admit\"`/`\"after_checkpoint\"`). This is the *only* place a hard-crash matrix is proven for real.\n\n**Egress-zero / socket proof**: `tests/runtime/test_plan26_egress.py` is real — `EgressGuard(receipts).install()`/`.uninstall()` monkeypatches `socket.socket.connect`/`connect_ex` at the class level (module `runtime/langgraph_factory/egress.py`), and tests literally attempt `socket.create_connection`, `urllib.request.urlopen`, `http.client.HTTPConnection.connect`, and raw `socket.socket().connect_ex(...)` and assert `EgressDenied`/receipted denial (`test_raw_socket_connect_is_denied_and_receipted`, `test_urllib_and_http_client_cannot_route_around_the_broker`, `test_connect_ex_is_also_brokered`, `test_direct_model_endpoint_is_denied` parametrized over `MODEL_API_HOSTS`). It does **not** test HTTP redirects or a literal \"sandbox-bypass\" scenario beyond the OS-level `sandbox-exec` proof living in `test_plan26_transport.py` (`tp.prove_workspace_isolation`, `tp.prove_transport_capabilities`) — DNS rebinding is covered (`test_dns_rebinding_to_an_unpinned_address_is_denied`), oversized-response and non-global-IP resolution are covered.\n\n**Lock-drift / CI proof**: `tests/runtime/test_plan26_lock_drift.py` asserts pin/lock/hash integrity (`test_direct_pins_file_declares_exact_versions`, `test_every_locked_distribution_carries_at_least_one_sha256_hash`, `test_lock_is_installable_with_require_hashes_semantics`, `test_forbidden_distributions_absent_from_lock`) and a **GitHub Actions workflow static-YAML check** (`test_workflow_fails_the_build_on_drift`, `test_workflow_installs_with_require_hashes`, `test_workflow_runs_both_plan26_dependency_tests`). It does not execute the graph; it's a pure supply-chain/CI-config gate. Not directly reusable for any of the 24 rows except as a citation for \"hash-locked environment\" boilerplate.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Per-test mapping\n\n### `test_no_nonproduct_success`\n- **Existing coverage**: `tests/runtime/test_plan26_unit_graph.py:1173` `test_no_node_in_this_path_can_emit_a_product_success_terminal`; `:1198` `test_the_model_path_uses_only_a_test_transport_and_no_product_output_root`; `tests/runtime/test_plan26_transport.py:786` `test_fake_transport_refuses_a_product_root`, `:793` `test_fake_transport_cannot_emit_a_terminal`; `tests/runtime/test_plan26_cli.py:311` `test_preflight_cannot_emit_product_success`; `tests/runtime/test_plan26_model_nodes.py:1122` `test_the_fake_transport_still_refuses_a_product_root`.\n- **Fixtures**: `_run_episode`/`_build_episode_fixture` (unit_graph.py); `tp.FakeCliTransport(sandbox_root=...)` refuses any root not under system temp (transport.py); `mn.build_test_model_node_context` (model_nodes.py).\n- **Verdict**: reuse-as-is as citations; **needs a thin wrapper test** in the new file that composes these into one adversarial narrative (graph+test-transport+prompt+capability+simulation \"presented as product\" all