ing (attack, not the happy path); check whether an explicit \"whole-unit regeneration attempt is refused\" negative test exists — `test_a_broad_repair_outside_its_boundary_is_refused` (247) is that negative case. **Verdict: reuse-as-is**, cite both `:220` and `:247`.\n\n### `test_repair_bound_reserved_first`\n- **Existing coverage**: `test_plan26_repair_acceptance.py:366` `test_attempt_bound_exhausts_before_a_fourth_repair_child`, `:385` `test_fingerprint_repeat_bound_exhausts_at_d17`; `test_plan26_workbook.py:468` `test_fingerprint_repeat_bound_exhausts_workbook_repair`, `:484` `test_attempt_bound_exhausts_before_a_fourth_repair_child` (workbook variant).\n- **Fixtures/API**: `repair.MAX_REPAIR_CHILDREN_PER_CHAIN`, `repair.MAX_FINGERPRINT_REPEATS`, `state[\"attempt_counters\"]` keyed `f\"repair|{unit}|{owner}|{fingerprint}\"` / `f\"repeat|{unit}|{owner}|{fingerprint}\"`.\n- **Verdict**: reuse-as-is for unit+workbook; note the \"reserved first\" (counter committed *before* activation) ordering claim is implicit in these tests (counter is pre-seeded, then D17/D18 run) rather than explicitly asserted as an ordering property — a genuinely new assertion of *ordering* (reserve-then-check vs check-then-reserve, i.e. that D90/D18 commits the counter in the same update that blocks the child) could strengthen this, but base behavior is proven. **needs-wrapper-test** if you want the literal ordering claim pinned.\n\n### `test_resume_preserves_accepted_bytes`\n- **Existing coverage**: `test_plan26_persistence.py::TestResumeRefusal` — `DRIFTS` tuple includes `(\"accepted_bytes\", \"accepted_byte_digests\", ...)` and `(\"accepted_bytes\", \"accepted_receipt_hashes\", ...)`; `test_every_drift_class_refuses_resume` (loops `DRIFTS`); `test_refused_resume_performs_no_product_work` (writes real bytes to `output_root/units/U01/unit.pdf`, snapshots with `_tree_snapshot`, asserts unchanged after `ResumeRefused`); `P.validate_resume_inputs(expected=, observed=)`.\n- **Verdict**: reuse-as