utes_the_bootstrap_spine_once_through_langgraph`) asserts `result[\"trace\"].count(\"D01_VALIDATE_AND_FREEZE_INPUTS\") == 1` and `\"D00R_REVALIDATE_RESUME_IDENTITY\" not in result[\"trace\"]` — but only for a **fresh** episode, not across a fresh→resume pair of the same run. `test_plan26_deterministic_nodes.py:1699` `test_d00r_admits_an_identical_resume_identity`, `:1734/1765/1788` D01 unit tests, `:1887/1920` D04 unit tests — all node-level, not a real two-episode graph run. `test_plan26_persistence.py::TestEpisodeLifecycle.test_each_episode_gets_a_new_thread_with_empty_langgraph_state` proves episode-2 gets a fresh empty thread and `bootstrap_kind == BOOTSTRAP_RESUME`, but doesn't run the *real compiled unit-path graph* through D01 for episode 2 to prove it's never re-entered, nor does it check `created_at` (a write-once global field) is unchanged.\n- **Verdict**: **needs-new-test.** Nothing currently runs `_run_episode` (or equivalent) twice against the same `output_root`/`run_id` — once fresh, once via `resume=True` with `P.prepare_episode_invocation(..., resume=True, read_view=...)` — and asserts (a) `trace.count(\"D01_VALIDATE_AND_FREEZE_INPUTS\")` stays `1` across the whole *run* (not just episode 1), (b) `state[\"created_at\"]` (or another declared write-once global field) is byte-identical after the resume episode, (c) `D00R` runs on the resume episode. Build this by composing `_build_episode_fixture` + `_run_episode` (episode 1, fresh) then a second `_prepare_episode`-style call with `resume=True` reusing `P.prepare_episode_invocation(output_root=..., lock=..., resume=True, read_view=P.ReadOnlyCheckpointView(compiled, saver))` from `test_plan26_persistence.py::TestEpisodeLifecycle`, then stream episode 2 and check `D01_VALIDATE_AND_FREEZE_INPUTS not in trace2` and cumulative count across both traces `== 1`. This genuinely needs both harnesses stitched together — the one piece nobody has combined yet.\n\n### `test_orphan_recovery_is_read_only_and_terminal_only`\n- **Existing coverage**: `test_plan26_persistence.py::TestOrphanRecovery` — `test_recovery_after_sigkill_uses_a_new_thread_and_touches_no_product` (real SIGKILL child `ORPHAN_CHILD`, `_tree_snapshot` before/after equality over product bytes), `test_recovery_services_raise_on_any_touch` (`P.build_recovery_services()` returns `renderer`/`source_retriever`/`transport_registry`, every attribute/call raises `P.RecoveryServiceForbidden`), `test_a_recovery_runtime_context_carries_only_forbidden_product_services`, `test_the_read_only_view_cannot_continue_a_prior_thread` (`P.ReadOnlyCheckpointView` has no `invoke`/`stream`/`update_state`/`ainvoke`/`batch`), `test_persistence_module_never_calls_invoke_or_update_state` (AST scan), `test_persistence_module_imports_no_product_or_model_module`. Also `test_plan26_unit_graph.py:1812` `test_a_hard_crash_is_recovered_as_an_orphan_without_continuing_its_thread` — uses `compiled.stream(...)`, breaks mid-stream after `D04_INITIALIZE_OR_RESUME`, then reads via `P.EpisodeLeaseLedger(...).open_lease()`, `P.ReadOnlyCheckpointView`, `P.extract_prior_episode`, asserts `readout.next` contains no `M0*` node ids.\n- **Gap vs spec row**: spec wants \"orphan recovery tries `invoke(None)`, transport, retrieval, render, or a saved product frontier → test fails before side effect; **only D00/D96/D98 execute**.\" The existing tests prove the *services* raise if touched and the *view* has no invoke method, but no test drives the real recovery episode (`invocation.bootstrap_kind == P.BOOTSTRAP_RECOVER_ORPHAN`) through the compiled graph and asserts the executed node trace is `{D00_BOOTSTRAP_EPISODE, D96_GRACEFUL_INTERRUPT_GATE, D98_WRITE_TERMINAL}` and nothing else (i.e., D91/D92 boundary — recall `routing.py:501` `route_initialize_or_resume`: \"fresh initialization to D05, validated resume import to D92\" — orphan recovery specifically must never reach D92 or beyond either).\n- **Verdict**: **needs-new-test** — compose `TestOrphanRecovery`'s real-SIGKILL child pattern with `_run_episode`-style streaming of the *actual* production graph in recovery mode, and assert trace ⊆ `{\"D00_BOOTSTRAP_EPISODE\", \"D96_GRACEFUL_INTERRUPT_GATE\", \"D98_WRITE_TERMINAL\"}`. Existing `test_a_hard_crash_is_recovered_as_an_orphan_without_continuing_its_thread` gets most of the way there but stops at *reading* the orphan, not *re-invoking* the compiled graph in recovery mode to completion.\n\n### `test_checkpoint_thread_and_namespace_contract`\n- **Existing coverage**: `test_plan26_persistence.py::TestCheckpointConfiguration` — `test_thread_ids_follow_the_frozen_format` (`P.episode_thread_id(\"run-x\", 1) == \"run-x:episode:000001\"`, `P.recovery_thread_id(\"run-x\", 7) == \"run-x:recover:7\"`, rejects ordinal `0`/`-1` via `P.PersistenceError`), `test_invoke_config_is_exactly_thread_id_and_root_namespace` (`P.invoke_config(...) == {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": ..., \"checkpoint_ns\": \"\"}}`, `P.CHECKPOINT_NS == \"\"`), `test_a_real_checkpoint_is_stored_under_the_root_namespace` (real SQL query `SELECT DISTINCT thread_id, checkpoint_ns FROM checkpoints`).\n- **Gap**: spec wants \"checkpoint namespace differs from root `\\\"\\\"`, or thread ID lacks run/episode relation → invocation rejected.\" No test currently constructs a *malformed* thread id (e.g. one with no `run_id` prefix, or a non-`\":episode:NNNNNN\"`/`\":recover:N\"` suffix) and asserts something explicitly *rejects invocation* (as opposed to merely computing the correct format). `P.episode_thread_id`/`recovery_thread_id` are pure formatters, not validators of externally-supplied thread ids.\n- **Verdict**: **needs-new-test** — check whether `runtime/langgraph_factory/persistence.py` has a validator for an *incoming* thread id/namespace (grep didn't surface one distinct from the formatters); if none exists, this row may need to assert the invariant is enforced structurally (only `episode_thread_id`/`recovery_thread_id`/`invoke_config` are ever used to build a `c